  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 253 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nThe upper level trough is expected to begin tracking across the West \nCoast by Wednesday, which bringing a pattern shift through the end \nof the week and into the weekend. As the trough slowly pushes east, \nthis will also set up a stream of lower level monsoonal moisture \nacross the region. With this push of monsoonal moisture, expect near \ndaily precipitation chances, with higher PWATs across the region. \nAlso, expect to see more seasonal highs on Wednesday, ranging from \nthe mid 80s to lower 90s across most of the region. \n\nHowever, before this pattern shift begins in earnest, will have to \ndeal with another potential round of severe weather Wednesday \nevening. In fact, SPC has already place western Nebraska in a Day 3 \nSlight Risk (Level 2 of 5). While some of the mesoscale features \nwill still need to be resolved to more accurately assess the severe \nthreat, expecting this will at least bring a risk for damaging wind \ngusts and large hail yet again. In addition to the potential severe \nrisk, expect to see higher PWAT values creep into the region, which \nwill favor the potential for heavy rainfall as well. With this in \nmind, the Weather Prediction Center has already highlighted the \nentire region with a Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall. In fact, \nWPC has a Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall Wednesday, Thursday, \nand Friday, given the push of monsoonal moisture. Will obviously be \nkeeping a close eye on this set up over the next few forecast cycles \nto better determine exact risks. However, for now, at least another \nnight of potentially strong to severe storms with heavy rainfall \nappears possible. \n\nThursday and into the weekend, will see quite a different weather \npattern compared to early this week. Highs late week are expected to \nbe around 10 to 15 degrees cooler than average, with highs mostly \nexpected to remain in the 70s. This will in large part be due to \nvery cloudy skies limiting daytime heating. However, there may also \nbe a push of cooler temperatures aloft across the Northern Plains \nreaching into portions of the region, which will further help limit \ntemperatures. The push of monsoonal moisture will remain across the \nregion, helping keep higher PWAT values across most of the region, \nand potentially bringing some beneficial rainfall across most of the \nregion. Ensemble guidance continues to suggest a respectable chance \nthat most of the region will receive at least one inch of total \nrainfall through the end of the week, with the European ensembles \nshowing a 60 to 70 percent chance of this occurring, and the GEFS \nshowing a 40 to 50 percent chance. Within this data, also seeing \nsome potential for higher amounts in some of the ensemble members. \nHowever, this does build confidence in daily precipitation chances \nlate week into the weekend, with the potential for some locally \nheavy amounts, as well.",
